HONESTY RULES (hard requirements):
- Every numeric value must be stated in the content or directly computable from it
  (counting items the content lists is fine). NEVER invent scores, ratings, rankings,
  or estimates the content does not state — a chart of made-up numbers is worse than
  no chart at all.
- Chart the SUBJECT of the content. Web-page furniture that leaked into scraped
  sources — navigation menus, sidebar sections, link counts, "edit"/"donate" items —
  is boilerplate, never data.
- If the content contains no genuine quantities worth charting, do not force one.
  Return exactly: {"chart": null, "reason": "<one short sentence saying what's missing>"}

DESIGN RULES:
- A denominator (such as 12 total questions) is NOT a performance target. Use a
  neutral bar or line chart for recall progress. Use Bullet Chart only when the
  source explicitly defines a goal/target and evaluative thresholds.
- Label fictional or illustrative quantities explicitly as "Not research evidence"
  in the chart title, using the same language as the content.
- Put source qualifications in input.caption, not only the title. For a fictional
  classroom example, state that the counts do not establish causation or a
  universal study schedule. Keep the caption brief and grounded in the source.
- Discrete counts need whole-number ticks; preserve the denominator and units.
  For counts out of a known total, add "denominator": N to that measure encoding
  (e.g. "y": {"field":"Correct answers (out of 12)","denominator":12}).
  This sets the full axis range without treating the total as a target.
- Every encoding channel must map to a DIFFERENT field. A color legend that duplicates
  the x axis carries no information — omit the channel instead.
- At least one encoded field must be a real measure with numeric values in the rows.
  Never build category-vs-category grids where each pair occurs once.
- Use at least 3 data rows unless the content genuinely only supports 2.
- Keep the chart focused on one comparison or trend. Write all text (title, labels) in
  the same language as the content.

